<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
        Removes generator-jhipster's second seeded account.

        `config/liquibase/data/user.csv` seeds two rows, not one: `admin` and `user`, both activated,
        both carrying a bcrypt hash of their own login as the password. Those hashes are in the
        generator, in this repository and in every clone of it.

        AdminAccountInitializer resets only `admin` from ABOFONSA_ADMIN_PASSWORD, so `user`/`user`
        stayed live wherever this was deployed. It holds ROLE_USER, and the security rules granted
        every authenticated principal the whole entity API — which made it a route to the waitlist
        table, its confirmation tokens, and write access to the content the public page renders.
        SecurityConfiguration now requires ROLE_ADMIN there, but that is the second lock; this is
        the first, and neither should be the only one.

        Why a new changeset rather than an edit to user.csv: the initial changeset is already
        applied everywhere, and Liquibase checksums `loadData` over the *contents* of the file it
        loaded. Editing the CSV changes that checksum and makes every existing database fail
        validation on next boot. The CSV is therefore left exactly as it is and the row is deleted
        after the fact — on a fresh database this inserts and then deletes, which is wasteful and
        correct.

        Deliberately not `context`-filtered. This must run under every profile, including test:
        the account is no more acceptable in CI than in production.
    -->
    <changeSet id="20260805110000-1" author="security-audit">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">select count(*) from jhi_user where login = 'user'</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>Delete the seeded 'user' account and its authority rows</comment>

        <delete tableName="jhi_user_authority">
            <where>user_id = (select id from jhi_user where login = 'user')</where>
        </delete>
        <delete tableName="jhi_user">
            <where>login = 'user'</where>
        </delete>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
